00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:02.140
Abraham: What exactly is threat modelling?

00:00:02.140 --> 00:00:05.700
Nicole: Threat modelling is an
overall risk-based approach to

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.799
identify and resolve any potential
threats and vulnerabilities that may

00:00:09.849 --> 00:00:12.139
compromise a business or organisation.

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:15.099
Abraham: There are many different
threat models and many different

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.290
frameworks involved but they all
follow similar steps and have the

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:23.670
same goal in preparing an organisation
or business on what might happen if

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:26.090
they fall prey to malicious attacks.

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:32.210
By proactively anticipating scenarios
that may impact an organisation they also

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:36.749
give an opportunity to look at what might
possibly happen even in extremes cases.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.350
Nicole: At the beginning of the
process, it is important to plan what

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.539
framework is needed for the threat model
and who and what it needs to cover.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:48.779
So, let’s look at an overall view of
the stages involved in threat modelling.

00:00:49.429 --> 00:00:53.459
The Discovery stage identifies
any desirable assets, any attack

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.970
vectors or threats surrounding them,
and how hackers may reach them.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:02.320
Abraham: The Implementation stage,
uses and analyses information

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.980
gathered in the discovery stage.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.149
This may be looking at potential
impact and how to prioritise the most

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:12.229
damaging attacks, and how to resolve the
vulnerabilities associated with these.

00:01:12.670 --> 00:01:16.449
Let’s look at these stages in more
detail starting with the discovery stage.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:21.200
Nicole: All assets including data and
applications should be catalogued.

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:25.999
This could be business assets, the
various data components and applications

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:30.170
that are necessary to run a business
and if targeted could disrupt the

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.580
activities of an organisation or business.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:38.149
It could also be data assets which could
be crypto mined to sell on the dark web.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:43.240
Abraham: Hackers are often opportunists
who look for easiest way in to exploit

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.770
already-known vulnerabilities, using tried
and tested routes such as unprotected

00:01:47.830 --> 00:01:50.140
admin accounts and default passwords.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:53.850
The areas at risk that hold the
data are the attack surface.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:57.679
By mapping out all the components
of these areas that communicate with

00:01:57.679 --> 00:02:02.150
the asset, threat modelling teams
can see how data flows to and from

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:04.290
the asset and any potential risks.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:09.180
Nicole: Identify the paths that
hackers use to reach these areas from

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.560
system penetration to asset removal.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:16.879
Hackers may have multiple ways to exploit
paths so these should be analysed also.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.810
This can be done by collecting
information on known exploits and

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.239
vulnerabilities that relate to each path.

00:02:23.959 --> 00:02:27.370
Abraham: This is where the good
actors or white hat hackers come in.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:30.770
Thinking like a hacker makes
it easier to stop an attack

00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:32.810
by being prepared and secure.

00:02:33.210 --> 00:02:36.829
So, we have looked at the discovery
stage, now let’s look at the

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.990
implementation stage in more detail.

00:02:39.820 --> 00:02:42.970
Nicole: The implementation stage
of threat modelling begins with a

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:47.350
comprehensive analysis of previously
gather discovery stage information.

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.030
Analysis of information collected
should be used to assess the

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.410
potential impact for each attack type.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:58.160
This should be done using any
assumptions made and any threat

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.010
intelligence or indicators.

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:05.080
As an attack can cause a chain of events
it’s important that the analysis covers

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.390
as much potential damage as possible.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:12.640
This may include reputation, leaked
data, legal action, general costs

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:16.940
to put things right such as relacing
equipment and the downtime that may

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.299
be caused of organisational services.

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.860
Abraham: Prioritisation is one of the
last steps of threat modelling which

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.970
prioritises previously discovered
vulnerabilities by focussing on the

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:29.950
most dangerous high impact attacks.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.250
Once these have been addressed then
a hierarchy would be followed to

00:03:34.250 --> 00:03:36.060
address the ‘less’ important threats.

00:03:36.809 --> 00:03:40.870
The final step is finding security
controls to mitigate all vulnerabilities

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.420
and remove and counter any threats.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:47.590
It is also the time when any
security gaps not discovered in

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:49.500
the initial stage can be addressed.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:54.390
Nicole: Threat modelling is important to
all businesses and organisations although

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:56.700
it can be costly and time consuming.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:01.490
It is often done manually by a competent
cyber security team but can also be

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:05.609
automated by using threat modelling
tools for those who don’t have a range

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.186
of cybersecurity personnel available.

00:04:07.186 --> 00:04:10.890
Abraham: How do we
evaluate a threat model?

00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:14.290
Nicole: Each threat model has
its own specific process and

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:17.530
sometimes threat models are used
in conjunction with each other.

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:23.370
Some focus on valuable assets, some more
on the types of attack, some on who uses

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.020
the system and some on prioritising.

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.450
But whichever model is chosen it
should meet the purpose and needs of

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:34.350
the organisation or business and the
strengths of their cyber security

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:38.590
team, and when it comes down to it,
a lot depends on what the business is

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.150
or what the organisation does to help
with the evaluation of suitability.

